 still works the same way, and the classic editor view is accessible. The Agents Window is an additional surface, not a forced replacement. Light users won't lose anything; they just won't gain much from the Cursor 3 changes either."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the /worktree command and why does it matter?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The /worktree command creates an isolated Git worktree — a separate filesystem checkout of your repository — before running an agent task. This means the agent's changes stay sandboxed until you choose to merge them. Without worktrees, parallel agents risk overwriting each other's edits; with worktrees, each agent operates in its own safe sandbox."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How does Cloud Agents billing work?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Cloud Agents require MAX mode and bill separately from your subscription's monthly credit pool on a per-minute VM basis. Cursor has not published the exact per-minute rate on its pricing page. Monitor your usage dashboard closely during initial Cloud Agent sessions before scaling up."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Should I switch from Windsurf to Cursor 3?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"If you value developer-controlled checkpoints and granular permission prompts, Cursor 3 is the stronger choice. If you prefer agents that act autonomously with minimal interruption — especially for unattended long-running tasks — Windsurf 2.0 with Cascade and Devin Cloud is more comfortable for that workflow. Both are $20/month."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is there a Cursor 4 yet?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. As of September 2026, Anysphere is still iterating on the 3.x line — the release train reached 3.11 in July 2026. The post-3.5 releases have focused on team management (multi-team support, Team MCPs, organization groups), customization (the 3.9 Customize page), and workflow depth (/automate triggers, side chats, transcript search) rather than another interface rebuild."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does the /automate command do?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Added in Cursor 3.8 (June 18, 2026), /automate creates agent automations that run on triggers instead of manual prompts — a GitHub event or a Slack message can start an agent task, and automations can use computer use to operate applications. It's the piece that makes unattended, event-driven agent workflows possible without leaving Cursor, though the same credit-burn caveats from parallel agents apply."}}]}</script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/_astro/Header.idkNhOdt.css">
